{{Quote|The Prime is One, but the Jedi are Many.|Kli the Elder in the ''Rammahgon''|<ref name="TROS Junior Novel">''[[Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker: A Junior Novel]]''</ref>}}
The '''Prime Jedi''' was an individual<ref name="TLJ-TVD" /> who was active during the [[Dawn of the Jedi era]].<ref name="Eras">{{SW|url=eras|text=Star Wars Eras}}</ref> Doing so while following an early version of the [[Jedi Code]],<ref name="Art Wars">{{InsiderCite|226|Art Wars}}</ref> the individual became the first [[Jedi]] to ever use [[the Force]].<ref name="SWCE 2023">{{SW|url=news/swce-2023-lucasfilm-studio-showcase|text=SWCE 2023: 11 Highlights from Lucasfilm's Studio Showcase}}</ref> Around [[25,025 BBY]],<ref name="Timelines">''[[Star Wars: Timelines]]''</ref> they discovered a [[Vergence|Force nexus]] on the remote [[planet]] [[Ahch-To]], where they built the [[first Jedi temple]]<ref name="GermanFanCite">{{GermanFanCite|101|Database}}</ref> and founded the [[Jedi Order]].<ref name="TLJ-TVD" /> Even after the temple was forgotten, a mosaic commemorating the first [[Grand Master]] was made.<ref name="GermanFanCite" /> It depicted the Prime [[Jedi]] in a state of [[meditation]],<ref name="TLJ-TVD" /> symbolically balancing [[Dark side of the Force|the dark]] and [[Light side of the Force|the light]] aspects of the Force.<ref name="GermanFanCite" />

The Prime Jedi was referenced in the ''[[Rammahgon]]'', one of the [[sacred Jedi texts]], by the ancient Jedi [[Kli the Elder]].<ref name="TROS Junior Novel" /> During the [[High Republic Era]], the [[Meditation Chamber]] located on the [[space station]] [[Starlight Beacon]] also featured a mosaic of the Prime Jedi.<ref name="The High Republic 7">[[The High Republic (2021) 7|''The High Republic'' (2021) 7]]</ref> The Prime Jedi's mosaic within the first Jedi Temple remained<ref name="Episode VIII">{{Film|VIII}}</ref> for thousands of years,<ref name="Timelines" /> even as the location became lost to the galaxy at large<ref name="Episode VII">{{Film|VII}}</ref> and home to the [[Exile (punishment)|exiled]] Jedi Master [[Luke Skywalker]].<ref name="Episode VIII" />

==Behind the scenes==
===Introduction into the ''Star Wars'' canon===
{{Quote|This is a symbol of the first Jedi, or 'Jedi Prime'. The center, yellowish rocks represent the energy of a lightsaber, splitting off into two directions: the dark and the light side of the Force. The black rocks symbolize the energy of the Force&mdash;everything in between, binding our universe together.|Seth Engstrom describing the mosaic of the Prime Jedi|<ref name="Art of TLJ">''[[The Art of Star Wars: The Last Jedi]]''</ref>}}
The Prime Jedi first appeared in a mosaic in the [[2017]] film [[Star Wars: Episode VIII The Last Jedi|''Star Wars'': Episode VIII ''The Last Jedi'']], the second installment of the ''[[Star Wars]]'' [[sequel trilogy]], written and directed by [[Rian Johnson]].<ref name="Episode VIII" /> They were first identified in [[Pablo Hidalgo]]'s reference book ''[[Star Wars: The Last Jedi: The Visual Dictionary]]'', which was released on the same day as the film.<ref name="TLJ-TVD" /> In the [[Star Wars Legends|''Star Wars'' Legends]] continuity, the founders of the [[Jedi Order/Legends|Jedi Order]] were [[Jedi Master/Legends|Jedi Masters]] [[Cala Brin]], [[Garon Jard]], [[Rajivari]], and [[Ters Sendon]].<ref name="SW:TOR">''[[Star Wars: The Old Republic]]''</ref>

The final design of the Prime Jedi mosaic was completed by concept artist [[Seth Engstrom]] in [[April]] [[2016]] after a long process of trial and error. The inspiration for the mosaic came from the Chinese symbology of [[Wikipedia:Yin and yang|yin and yang]]&mdash;dark and light shapes within each other, which according to ''[[The Art of Star Wars: The Last Jedi]]'' is understood through [[Wikipedia:Taoism|Taoism]] as "representing both the balance of dualities and that the schism between them is merely perceptual."<ref name="Art of TLJ" />

===Initial ideas and the Jedi's backstory===
{{Quote|When I first talked to Kathy Kennedy about it, I just said, ‘I just see this opening to make kind of a ‘Ben-Hur' or ‘The 10 Commandments' about the birth of the force.' The force has become a kind of religious legend that spans through all these movies. But where did it come from? How is it found? Who found it? Who was the first Jedi? And that's what I'm writing right now.|James Mangold about his influences, like the Prime Jedi, for his ''Star Wars'' movie|<ref name="MangoldsStoryPlans" />}}
The idea of delving into the origins of the [[Jedi Order]] harkens back to the initial plans ''Star Wars'' creator [[George Lucas]] had for the [[1999]] film {{Film|I}}, according to [[original trilogy]] producer [[Gary Kurtz]], back when the [[Star Wars saga|''Star Wars'' saga]] was meant to be comprised of nine films in the early [[1970]]s. However, much to Kurtz's dismay, Lucas changed his mind in [[1980]] and opted to wrap up the series with the [[1983]] film {{Film|VI}} before reconsidering his stance years later, but coming up with a different story than the one he originally had for the [[prequel trilogy]].<ref name="TFN Gary Kurtz's Interview">{{WebCite|url=http://www.theforce.net/latestnews/story/gary_kurtz_reveals_original_plans_for_episodes_19_80270.asp|text=TheForce.Net - Latest News - Interviews - Gary Kurtz Reveals Original Plans for Episodes 1-9|author=TheForce.net|archivedate=20200107091056}}</ref>

On [[February 6]], [[2018]], [[Wikipedia:Game of Thrones (TV series)|''Game of Thrones'']] showrunners [[David Benioff]] and [[D.B. Weiss]] were announced by [[StarWars.com]] to produce and write a [[Untitled Star Wars film series|''Star Wars'' film series]],<ref name="Benioff&Weiss">{{SW|url=news/game-of-thrones-creators-david-benioff-and-d-b-weiss-to-write-and-produce-a-new-series-of-star-wars-films|text=''Game of Thrones'' Creators David Benioff and D.B. Weiss to Write and Produce a New Series of ''Star Wars'' Films|nolive=1}}</ref> which reportedly focused on the time period in which the [[Jedi]] came to exist, due to the duo's apparent interest in that untouced era of the ''Star Wars'' [[canon]].<ref name="VarietysClaims">{{WebCite|url=https://variety.com/2019/film/features/star-wars-kathleen-kennedy-david-benioff-db-weiss-1203388611|text='Star Wars': Inside 'Game of Thrones' Creators' Exit and the Pressures Facing Lucasfilm (EXCLUSIVE)|author=Lang, Brent|date=2019-10-30|archivedate=20200721105505}}</ref> However, due to their scheduling conflicts with [[Netflix]], Benioff and Weiss reluctantly walked away from their project on [[October 28]], [[2019]], though [[Lucasfilm]] showed a willigness to work with the duo again in the future.<ref name="DeadlinesExitNews">{{WebCite|url=https://deadline.com/2019/10/star-wars-setback-game-of-thrones-duo-david-benioff-d-b-weiss-exit-trilogy-1202771184|text='Star Wars' Setback: 'Game Of Thrones' Duo David Benioff & D.B. Weiss Exit Trilogy|author=Boucher, Geoff|date=2019-10-28|archivedate=20200721195000}}</ref>

On [[April 7]], [[2023]], it was confirmed during [[Celebration Europe (2023)|Celebration Europe]] that [[James Mangold]] would direct an [[Untitled Star Wars film (James Mangold)|untitled ''Star Wars'' film]] centered on the origins of the Jedi.<ref name="New movies" /> According to Mangold after his panel, how [[The Force]] was discovered and found by the first of the Jedi was one of the things that drew him to the project.<ref name="MangoldsStoryPlans">{{WebCite|url=https://variety.com/2023/film/news/star-wars-movies-dave-filoni-james-mangold-1235576542|text=‘Star Wars' Movies: James Mangold, Dave Filoni to Direct New Films|author=Ravindran, Manori|date=2023-04-07|archivedate=20230408005824}}</ref><ref name="New movies">{{SW|url=news/swce-2023-new-star-wars-films|text=SWCE 2023: Three New ''Star Wars'' Movies Announced}}</ref>

